// The sections of the English guide about goroutines and channels; guide.rk lists them all.

import { example } from "../example.rk"

export comp Async() {
    return <>
        <p>
            <code>async func</code> returns a Promise, and <code>await</code> waits for it. The result
            types are written as in any function, several of them too: an
            {" "}<code>async func load() (string, error)</code> returns
            {" "}<code>{"Promise<(string, error)>"}</code>, and <code>await</code> unpacks the values
            back. A module can <code>await</code> at the top level, and an event handler can be
            async: a change of state after <code>await</code> updates the markup.
        </p>
        {example("async")}
    </>
}

export comp Goroutines() {
    return <>
        <p>
            JavaScript runs a program in one thread: a long computation stops everything else, and a
            worker takes a file of its own and messages written by hand. In Rook it is one
            word, as in Go, and the compiler checks what goes to the other thread.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>go f(x)</code> calls a function as a goroutine, in another thread, and gives a
            Promise of the result. Goroutines run at once on different processors: the threads are a
            pool of workers, Web Workers in the browser and worker threads in Node, up to one per
            processor. A goroutine shares no memory with the program: it gets copies of the
            arguments and sends back a copy of the result, so they are data, and the function cannot
            use the <code>let</code> variables of the module. The compiler checks both.
        </p>
        {example("goroutines")}
        <p>
            <code>{"go func(n number) { ... }(i)"}</code> runs a function literal as a goroutine: the
            local names it uses are copied to it, like its arguments. <code>transfer(x)</code> moves
            an <code>ArrayBuffer</code> or a typed array to the goroutine instead of copying it, and
            leaves it empty here. <code>go</code> is for work that keeps a processor busy; to wait
            for a timer or the network, call an async function without <code>await</code>: it runs
            at once in the same thread.
        </p>
        {example("go-func")}
        <p>
            <code>{"go for i, v in items { ... }"}</code> is a parallel loop: the array is split into
            parts, one per processor, each part runs as a goroutine, and the loop waits for all of
            them. A worker gets only its part, so the body writes only its own element,
            {" "}<code>items[i]</code>, and reads other elements from another array; the other names
            it uses are copied to it, as to a function literal.
        </p>
        {example("go-for")}
    </>
}

export comp Channels() {
    return <>
        <p>
            Channels pass values between goroutines, as in Go: <code>{"chan<number>(10)"}</code> makes a
            channel with room for ten numbers, <code>{"ch <- v"}</code> sends a value and waits
            until the channel takes it, <code>{"<-ch"}</code> receives one, and
            {" "}<code>for v in ch</code> receives them until <code>close(ch)</code>. Both ends
            wait, so they work in async functions. A channel can go to goroutines in other threads:
            what passes through it is copied. <code>{"<-chan number"}</code> only receives and <code>{"chan<- string"}</code> only sends: a function that takes one
            cannot use it the other way.
        </p>
        {example("channels")}
    </>
}

export comp Select() {
    return <>
        <p>
            <code>select</code> waits for the first of several operations on channels and runs its
            case, as in Go: <code>{"case text = <-fast:"}</code> receives into a new variable of
            the case, <code>{"case out <- v:"}</code> sends, and <code>default</code> runs when
            nothing is ready at once. <code>after(ms)</code> is a channel that gets the time once,
            after <code>ms</code>, so it makes a timeout, and a case whose channel is
            {" "}<code>null</code> never goes on.
        </p>
        {example("select")}
        <p>
            <code>tick(ms)</code> gets the time every <code>ms</code> until
            {" "}<code>close(ticks)</code>. <code>cancel(task)</code> stops a goroutine, given the
            Promise of <code>go</code>: it stops at once, even in the middle of a loop, and
            the Promise fails with "goroutine cancelled".
        </p>
        {example("timers")}
    </>
}

export comp Streams() {
    return <>
        <p>
            <code>chan(stream, size)</code> turns a <code>ReadableStream</code>, such as the body
            of a <code>fetch</code> response, into a channel of its chunks, and gives a second
            channel that gets the error of the stream, or <code>null</code> at its end. The next
            chunk is read only when the last one is in the channel, so the room of the channel
            is the backpressure of the stream, and a goroutine in another thread can read it.
            {" "}<code>close(chunks)</code> cancels the stream.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>stream(ch)</code> is the other way: a <code>ReadableStream</code> of the values
            of a channel, which ends when the channel is closed, to give as the body of a
            {" "}<code>Response</code> or to <code>pipeTo</code> somewhere.
        </p>
        {example("streams")}
    </>
}
